        ":::\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Generate scatter plot in Flourish\n",
        "\n",
        "Select *Scatter* in the catalog of visualizations.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectedScatter.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Next, load the \"auto_dataset.xlsx\" into Flourish. Set the variables under study `mpg` and `weight` and assign them to **Y values** and **X values**, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/LoadedAutoHighlight.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Go back to the **Preview** tab and add text to the axes and title.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FinalScatterPlot.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Plots for three or four variables\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "- When examining a distribution or relationship, we often want to compare it across data subgroups.\n",
        "\n",
        "- This process of conditioning on additional variables leads to visualizations involving three or more variables.\n",
        "\n",
        "- We now illustrate how to extend the scatter plot to visualize multiple variables.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Scatter plot by color\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-scatter data-src=\"visualisation/26496861\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26496861/thumbnail\" alt=\"scatter visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Add color to the scatter plot\n",
        "\n",
        "We a categorical variable in the **Color** section. For example, let's use `origin`.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectScatterbyColor.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## The result\n",
        "\n",
        "Scatter plots by color work well when the additional variable $Z$ is categorical with few categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FinalScatterPlotbyColor.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bubble Plot: Scatter for 4 variables\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-scatter data-src=\"visualisation/26497298\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26497298/thumbnail\" alt=\"scatter visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Add bubbles to the scatter plot\n",
        "\n",
        "We set a variable in the **Size** section, say, `horsepower`.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectBubblePlot.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Resulting bubble plot\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/InitialBubblePlotAuto.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Improve the bubble plot\n",
        "\n",
        "We can enhance the plot by reducing the minimum and maximum sizes for the bubbles. Let's set the maximum size to 200.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/AdjustBubblePlot.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Final bubble plot\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FinalBubblePlot.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Line plot\n",
        "\n",
        "A line plot is a visual representation of data where data points are connected by a line. Axes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $X$ (horizontal): Represents time or the independent variable.\n",
        "- $Y$ (vertical): Represents the dependent variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each point represents a value at a given moment.\n",
        "\n",
        ". . .\n",
        "\n",
        "> The objective is to explore trends over time or the evolution of a continuous or discrete variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example 2\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the data in the file \"spotify.xlsx\". This dataset contains the global daily streams of the top five most popular songs on the music streaming service Spotify in 2017.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SpotifyDataset.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Line plot\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-chart data-src=\"visualisation/26497776\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26497776/thumbnail\" alt=\"chart visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Generate a line plot in Flourish\n",
        "\n",
        "Select *Line chart* in the catalog of visualizations.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectedLine.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Next, load the \"auto_dataset.xlsx\" into Flourish. Set the variables under study `Date` and `Despacito` and assign them to **Labels/time** and **Values**, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/LoadedDespacitoHighlight.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Go back to the **Preview** tab and add text to the axes and title.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FonsiLine.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Multiple line charts\n",
        "\n",
        "We can visualize multiple variables simultaneously using a line plot. For example, we can show the evolution of play counts for the 5 songs in the file \"spotify.xlsx\" over time.\n",
        "\n",
        "To this end, simply go back to the **Data** tab and set a range of variables in **Values**.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/RangeLineChart.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-chart data-src=\"visualisation/26430359\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26430359/thumbnail\" alt=\"chart visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plots for Numerical and Categorical Variables\n",
        "\n",
        "## Plotting the average by groups\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can summarize the values of the numerical variable $Y$ for each category of a categorical variable $X$ using the *mean* or *average*.\n",
        "\n",
        "- For example, let's plot the average miles per gallon of cars produced in America, Europe, and Japan.\n",
        "\n",
        "- A common visualization for plotting a numerical and a discrete variable when there is only one value per category is the **bar plot**.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The bar plot encodes quantitative data across different categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bar plot for averages\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-chart data-src=\"visualisation/26430455\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26430455/thumbnail\" alt=\"chart visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Generate bar plot in Flourish\n",
        "\n",
        "Select *Bar chart* in the catalog of visualizations.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectBarChart.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Load the \"auto_dataset.xlsx\" into Flourish. Set the variables under study `origin` and `mpg` and assign them to **Labels/time** and **Values**, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/BarChartMPGSelect.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Go to the **Preview** tab and set *Average* in **Aggregation mode**.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FormatBarChartMPG.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Beeswarm plot\n",
        "\n",
        "A **beeswarm plot** shows every observation as a point and places the points so they do not overlap.\n",
        "\n",
        "It allows us to see both the [**individual observations**]{style=\"color:darkblue;\"} and the [**distribution**]{style=\"color:darkblue;\"} of the numerical variable within each category.\n",
        "\n",
        ". . .\n",
        "\n",
        "**Why use it?**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Easy to see the actual observations.\n",
        "- Makes differences between groups visible.\n",
        "- Works particularly well with small and medium-sized datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Beeswarm plot\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-chart data-src=\"visualisation/30113726\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<div class=\"flourish-embed flourish-scatter\" data-src=\"visualisation/30113726\"><script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/30113726/thumbnail\" alt=\"scatter visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        "\n",
        "</div>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Creating a beeswarm plot in Flourish\n",
        "\n",
        "Select *Beeswarm* in the catalog of visualizations.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/beeswarmOne.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Load the \"auto_dataset.xlsx\" into Flourish. Set the variables under study `origin` and `mpg` and assign them to **X** and **Y**, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/beeswarmTwo.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Improve the plot by adding a title and axis labels.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/beeswarmThree.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bar chart or beeswarm plot?\n",
        "\n",
        "| **Bar chart of averages** | **Beeswarm plot** |\n",
        "|:-----------------------------------|:-----------------------------------|\n",
        "| Shows **one value per category**: the mean. | Shows **every observation** within each category. |\n",
        "| Makes it easy to compare the **average** between groups. | Allows us to compare the **distribution** between groups. |\n",
        "| Hides variation within each category. | Shows variation, concentration, gaps, and unusual observations. |\n",
        "\n",
        "## What should we look for?\n",
        "\n",
        "- In a bar chart, focus on the differences between the [**average values**]{style=\"color:darkblue;\"} of the categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "- In a beeswarm plot, look at how much the [**distributions overlap**]{style=\"color:darkblue;\"}. Little or no overlap suggests that the categorical variable can clearly [**distinguish between values**]{style=\"color:darkblue;\"} of the numerical variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "- A large difference in averages does *not necessarily* mean that the groups are completely different. The beeswarm plot can reveal whether their individual observations actually overlap.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Plots for Categorical Variables\n",
        "\n",
        "## Two Categorical Variables\n",
        "\n",
        "With two categorical variables, we compare the distribution of one variable across subgroups defined by the other variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "In fact, we keep one variable constant and plot the distribution of the other.\n",
        "\n",
        ". . .\n",
        "\n",
        "To do this, there are old and new plots:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Hierarchical bar plot\n",
        "- Tree map plot\n",
        "- Circle packing plot\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example 3\n",
        "\n",
        "As an example, let's consider the data in the file \"penguins.xlsx\" again.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/PenguinsDataset.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "For instance, let's study the distribution of penguin species across the three different islands using an stacked bar chart.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Hierarchical bar plot\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-hierarchy data-src=\"visualisation/26498177\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26498177/thumbnail\" alt=\"hierarchy visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Create a hierarchical plot in Flourish\n",
        "\n",
        "Search and select the plot in the catalog of visualizations.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectHierarchyCatalog.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "After you load the \"penguins.xlsx\" data, select two categorical variables in the **Categories/nesting** section.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/SelectVarsHierarhicalPlot.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "To enhance the plot, switch off the automatic height\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/AutomaticOffHierarhicalBar.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Final hierarchical bar plot with titles\n",
        "\n",
        "The chart shows the frequency of species and island name.\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/FinalHierarchyChart.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## If you change hierarchy to tree\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/TreeMapSelect.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Tree map plot\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-hierarchy data-src=\"visualisation/26498305\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26498305/thumbnail\" alt=\"hierarchy visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## If you change hierarchy to circles\n",
        "\n",
        "![](images/CircleMapSelect.png){fig-align=\"center\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Circle packing plot\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.flourish-embed .flourish-hierarchy data-src=\"visualisation/26498314\"}\n",
        "```{=html}\n",
        "<script src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/resources/embed.js\"></script>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "<noscript><img src=\"https://public.flourish.studio/visualisation/26498314/thumbnail\" alt=\"hierarchy visualization\" width=\"100%\"/></noscript>\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Discussion\n",
        "\n",
        "Visualizations such as hierarchy bars, tree maps, and circle packing plots help us explore how two categorical variables relate through *nested structure*.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Hierarchy bar charts** are easy to read when one category has only a few levels. The show a clear sense of order from parent → child categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Tree maps** are excellent use of space when there are many subcategories. The area encodes quantity, helping highlight dominant groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Circle packing plots** are more visually appealing and intuitive for showing nested groups. They are helpful when emphasizing *clusters* rather than exact magnitudes.\n",
        "\n",
